#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501275} :activity/id #uuid "0a41176c-b39d-554a-935e-72997f04c98e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050555 :topic/id #uuid "52ee4eec-32eb-50f8-908d-64e67d09f541" :topic/math-academy-id 1938} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501247 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501201 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13f0ad0a-171c-5322-8cf2-cfca08972fdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{e\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500510} {:answer/id #uuid "407711da-42ac-56dc-a3f6-af974631b602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500594} {:answer/id #uuid "5490207d-8efb-5f97-80cd-1bd0f27685fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{e\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500639} {:answer/id #uuid "86e21ca8-366b-5985-a073-18a5cd00683d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500749} {:answer/id #uuid "8d4c7ab4-1278-5730-804a-3c97ca86d365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{e\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500762}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500639} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcae1503-ee5b-5bcc-859b-64edce5a1f65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501132}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a3fef551-e9bd-56f6-a5fb-6131de39b9cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-86542" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)=\\frac{{e}^{y}}{yx}.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=⟨2,4⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of $v$ is equal to the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,1).$ \n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}}=\\sqrt{20}=2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}⟨2,4⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{e}^{y}}{yx}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{e}^{y}}{yx})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}y},\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}(y-1)}{x{y}^{2}}⟩ \\\\ ∇g(1,1) & =⟨-\\frac{{e}^{1}}{{1}^{2}\\cdot 1},\\,\\frac{{e}^{1}\\cdot (1-1)}{1\\cdot {1}^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-e,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(1,1) & =∇g(1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨-e,0⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨1,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}(-e\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{e}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{e\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0a60e94a-7f5d-54b3-9400-bd9ba416bdb2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501277}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501275 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323875 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8837b541-d3d8-5ebe-8ea8-af61591cb6fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322232} {:answer/id #uuid "b6f03f12-e1ac-5472-a11e-f1f87bcd9621" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322572} {:answer/id #uuid "c1717e43-53c5-54f5-8c5f-68df4b9cfd08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322650} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a54f97-4039-5587-a94f-458a55a1776d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322792} {:answer/id #uuid "f7ef68e3-e12b-57d6-890b-83987a1922bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323097}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322792} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a40605a-36ce-555d-abab-e5a81e672aeb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3a0c033d-5079-51f1-a44e-c13c59694293" :question/math-academy-id "q-37083" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=(x+y)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y).$ What is the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(2,-1)$ in the direction in which the function decreases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the opposite direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇\\,f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x+y)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x+y)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+1)\\text{ }i+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+1)\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇\\,f(2,-1) & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-1)+1)i-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-1)+1)j \\\\  & =-i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(2,-1)$ in the direction of $-∇f(2,-1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-∇\\,f(2,-1)‖ & =‖-i-j‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7cf532da-0f94-51c8-af70-3482c03bc391" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501291}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501277 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501150 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "172f3f98-43e9-586f-8bdf-a9ddf4ae061e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\,i-12\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500519} {:answer/id #uuid "6e5dcfde-da03-5e9b-8257-b3a15610fd0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\,i+18\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500690} {:answer/id #uuid "9305e79c-d3f6-5049-b273-0bc89befd2f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\,i+4\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500772} {:answer/id #uuid "9b2acabe-a8aa-5bd5-a28c-992a7b2ef314" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5\\,i+3\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500782} {:answer/id #uuid "e95111ed-e4a8-5a81-b040-27eaaa405bc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i+9\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500966}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500690} :answer-field/id #uuid "35227601-10b3-594c-aac4-a002a6f61ca3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501037}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1c3c8fd2-c84f-5ffa-82e1-4f53ac4509f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-130277" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=2(xy+1)^{3/2}$ increase most rapidly at the point $(3,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2(xy+1)^{3/2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2(xy+1)^{3/2})\\,j \\\\  & =3y\\sqrt{xy+1}\\,i+3x\\sqrt{xy+1}\\,j \\\\ ∇f(3,1) & =3(1)\\sqrt{(3)(1)+1}\\,i+3(3)\\sqrt{(3)(1)+1}\\,j \\\\  & =6\\,i+18\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $6\\,i+18\\,j.$"} :step/id #uuid "892b58dc-320d-53ce-b8f0-06a4365c54d1"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501291 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501227 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08fb6012-956b-5c39-8935-74645f89ee82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}\\,i+2\\pi \\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500483} {:answer/id #uuid "9f0516fc-fa33-5a32-bc85-021583caaccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}\\,i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500790} {:answer/id #uuid "a91d4692-51f1-5ef6-96a3-68a85bcad337" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i-\\pi \\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500817} {:answer/id #uuid "b20c5f55-306b-5cef-9a67-804b11be245c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i-{\\pi }^{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500840} {:answer/id #uuid "f8c25011-b246-57b2-afb4-9949bd083e31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi \\,i+\\pi \\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500997}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500840} :answer-field/id #uuid "941d1058-20a9-5c97-a5b7-7c5da913add0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501084}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d2926681-2f02-501e-9c4c-db877ffeff07" :question/math-academy-id "q-83562" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}y$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(\\pi ,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(\\pi ,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}y)\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+2xy)\\text{ }i-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{x}^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇f(\\pi ,0) & =-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡0+2\\cdot \\pi \\cdot 0)\\text{ }i+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡0+{\\pi }^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =i-{\\pi }^{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $i-{\\pi }^{2}\\,j.$"} :step/id #uuid "d394d923-4ab3-59e3-94fd-7981e3aa060d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501247}}] :activity/title "Directional Derivatives" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500458}]}
